Comment (frontend/src/lib/api.ts:38):
`request()` invokes `onUnauthorized()` for every HTTP 401, including unauthenticated calls like `/api/login` where a 401 typically means "invalid credentials" rather than an expired session. In the current UI this leads to showing the session-expired message instead of the backend error. Consider only calling `onUnauthorized()` when an Authorization token was actually sent (e.g., `if (response.status === 401 && token)`), or add a per-request flag to skip the unauthorized handler for login/register flows.

---
Comment (frontend/src/lib/api.ts:64):
`register()` is typed as returning only `{ message: string }`, but the backend returns both `message` and `user` in the JSON body (201 Created). Even though the caller currently ignores the response, this mismatch makes the client typing misleading and can cause confusion when the response is used later. Consider introducing a `RegisterResponse` type that matches the API (including `user`) or changing the return type to match the actual payload.
---
Comment (frontend/src/App.vue:111):
The `try` block in `submitAuth()` performs both `register` and `login`, but the `catch` fallback message for register mode is always "Unable to register.". If registration succeeds but the subsequent login fails, this fallback becomes inaccurate. Consider separating the error handling (or using distinct fallback messages) so the user gets an appropriate message depending on which step failed.

Implementation guidance: In frontend/src/lib/api.ts, update createApiClient.request() so the global onUnauthorized handler is not invoked for unauthenticated auth flows such as login/register invalid-credential 401s. Add a small internal request options type, for example RequestInit & { skipUnauthorizedHandler?: boolean }, destructure skipUnauthorizedHandler out before calling fetch so it is not passed to fetch(), and change the 401 handling to call onUnauthorized() only when response.status === 401, a token was actually present/sent, and skipUnauthorizedHandler is not true. Pass { skipUnauthorizedHandler: true } from login() and register(). Keep throwing ApiError with readError(response) unchanged so the UI can show the backend invalid-credentials message.
Implementation guidance: In frontend/src/types/api.ts, add an exported RegisterResponse interface that matches the backend response shape: message: string and user: User. Then in frontend/src/lib/api.ts import RegisterResponse alongside the existing ApiErrorBody/LoginResponse/Todo types and change register() from request<{ message: string }>("/api/register", ...) to request<RegisterResponse>("/api/register", ...). Do not change App.vue behavior; the caller may continue ignoring the returned value.
Implementation guidance: In frontend/src/App.vue, adjust submitAuth() so registration and the automatic login after registration have distinct fallback messages. Keep the current username trimming and validation. One straightforward approach is to introduce let fallback = authMode.value === "register" ? "Unable to register." : "Unable to sign in." before the try, then after a successful api.register(username, authForm.password) set fallback = "Account created, but unable to sign in." before calling api.login(...). In the catch block call setError(error, fallback). Preserve the existing success status messages and authLoading finally behavior.
